31
00:03:04,202 --> 00:03:13,140
Rym: One of the pins would ask for too much power for one part of the chip and that part of the chip would eventually degrade to the point that it would start erroring.

32
00:03:13,302 --> 00:03:14,219
Rym: and then the chip is fucked.

33
00:03:15,206 --> 00:03:15,433
Scott: Mmm.

34
00:03:16,262 --> 00:03:32,320
Rym: And the fix is out, and basically, if you have an Intel chip that's affected and you have any even remotely reasonable motherboard, whatever the current BIOS update is, just fixes this, prevents the problem, total fix, locked in.

35
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:34,468
Scott: What if you had some amount of degradation?

36
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:40,699
Scott: Obviously, the patch will prevent further degradation, but, you know, are you at the point where...

37
00:03:41,881 --> 00:03:47,860
Rym: So if you have any detectable degradation, meaning system instability, your chip's fucked.

38
00:03:48,801 --> 00:03:50,688
Rym: There's no way back that can't be repaired.

39
00:03:50,949 --> 00:03:54,240
Rym: It has physically destroyed itself in a way that cannot be corrected.

40
00:03:55,182 --> 00:04:00,320
Rym: Your CPU could never be more stable than it is the day after you install the new microcode.

41
00:04:01,581 --> 00:04:31,116
Rym: But Intel, one, contrary to what a lot of AMD people were saying all over the internet, Intel fully admitted the problem, announced every chip that's affected, or even could be affected, has a blanket two-year extension to whatever the warranty was, specifically for this but covering everything, and they are actually working on a tool that you'll be able to run to detect if your chip has any degradation, in which case, they'll also replace it.

42
00:04:34,863 --> 00:04:34,946
Rym: Mmm.

43
00:04:37,083 --> 00:04:55,160
Rym: Many people who haven't messed with PCs and workstations that much lately may remember that for much of human history, installing BIOS updates, especially big ones that also update things like CPU microcode, was a dicey process, at least for ASUS motherboards.

44
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:58,400
