166
00:16:07,401 --> 00:16:16,980
Scott: That's how granular they got with this plan here of how to make Microsoft, the company and its products actually secure for one.

167
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:20,640
Rym: So I might be able to shed some light onto why this is happening.

168
00:16:21,662 --> 00:16:23,700
Rym: And it's a little bit bigger than just CrowdStrike.

169
00:16:24,321 --> 00:16:29,320
Rym: So there's a regulation coming in Europe that goes into effect in January next year.

170
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:31,757
Rym: Like it's now called DORA, D-O-R-A.

171
00:16:32,761 --> 00:16:34,900
Rym: And there's similar regulations.

172
00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:39,400
Rym: There's all European and United Kingdom regulations that are coming.

173
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:39,942
Rym: Like the U.S.

174
00:16:39,962 --> 00:16:42,298
Rym: doesn't have anything like this yet that I'm aware of.

175
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:49,520
Rym: But these regulations basically broadly, I'm not giving anyone advice on how to respond to regulations to be clear.

176
00:16:50,461 --> 00:17:09,200
Rym: They broadly say that if you are a regulated entity in Europe, meaning like banks, a lot of financial institutions, then you must look at not just your own operational resilience, but you have to look at third parties you rely on for critical things.

177
00:17:09,682 --> 00:17:20,000
Rym: And I would argue and point out that the Microsoft operating system is definitely something that major regulated entities around the world use for very critical activities.

178
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:31,140
Rym: And thus Microsoft will be subject to some amount of scrutiny likely as European entities must then work with auditors to prove their operational resilience, including their relationships with third parties.

179
00:17:31,661 --> 00:17:36,400
